\section{Multi-touch}
\subsection{Introduction}
The way in which we interact with computers has changed very little over the last few decades~\citep{Kin09}. That is, a keyboard and mouse remain the primary means through which individuals interact with graphical user interfaces~\citep{Kin09}. These techniques, however, greatly limit both the number of simultaneous input points as well as, due to this, the types of interfaces that can be developed~\citep{Mosc08}. In addition, this distances the user by limiting the degree and magnitude with which they can physically interact with the system~\citep{Mosc06}. Multi-touch technology, however, offers an alternative to the traditional computing paradigm~\citep{Kin09}. Multi-touch, in relation to touch-screen devices, refers to the ability to simultaneously control two or more input points or touches~\citep{Jaim07}. These systems allow users to physically interact with the computing device with the option to use two or more input points~\citep{Mosc08}. This in turn, increases the degrees of freedom that the user is able to manipulate, which has implications for the type of applications that are possible and the way in which interaction itself is performed~\citep{Mosc08}.


\subsection{Types of interaction}
At present, there is no standard hardware with which multi-touch interfaces are developed~\citep{Jaim07}. Some devices utilise capacitive technology, while others use cameras and computer vision techniques in order to provide touch capabilities~\citep{Wigd09}. Due to these variations, there are a number of different variations in the input techniques in use. These include direct-touch versus indirect-touch, single hand versus bimanual as well as variations on the number of fingers in use~\citep{Kin09}. There are also techniques where the shape and orientation of the user's whole hand is tracked and identified~\citep{Wu03}. These techniques will be discussed in further detail below.

\subsubsection{Direct touch versus indirect touch}
Indirect touch refers to the use of some mapping of a pointing device to control and manipulate objects on screen~\citep{Mosc08}. Users are required to visually track this pointer or cursor~\citep{Kin09}. Direct touch, in contrast, refers to directly touching or interacting with the object one wishes to manipulate~\citep{Kin09}. Thus one tracks the finger or fingers interacting with the object rather than an onscreen cursor~\citep{MoscH06}. 

Perhaps the most well known example of indirect touch is a mouse~\citep{Kin09}. However, research has also been conducted on using multiple fingers in order to control a number of control points in an indirect manner~\citep{Mosc08,MoscH06}. In this scenario one is able to manipulate a number of onscreen control points through the use of multiple fingers. However, this manipulation is done on a device connected to a seperate output screen rather than on the actual display screen itself~\citep{MoscH06}.

Direct touch has been shown to be faster than using single point, indirect touch methods, such as a mouse~\citep{Kin09}. However, no comparison for speed has yet been performed between direct touch and indirect, multi-touch based methods. Direct touch, however, suffers from issues of occlusion~\citep{Mosc08}. That is, when interacting with an object, one often hides or blocks portions of this object~\citep{Kin09}. This is of concern for interfaces with small buttons and other such elements~\citep{Kin09}. In addition, due to the size of a finger relative to that of a mouse cursor, both touch-based direct and indirect methods suffer from decreased precision of selection~\citep{Kin09}. However, as mentioned above, the nature of touch-based interaction allows for a greater number of control points, and thus degrees of freedom within interaction than do conventional mouse-based pointing devices.

\subsubsection{Multifinger techniques}
Many touch devices support multiple points of input. This increases the degrees of freedom that a user has when manipulating or controlling an object or interface as they are able to use multiple fingers instead of just a single pointer~\citep{Mosc08}.

However, some research has found that using multiple fingers on the same hand often produces a minimal increase in the degrees of freedom the user is able to control~\citep{Mosc08}. This is due to the fact that fingers of the same hand are both physically and neurologically linked~\citep{Mosc06}. Thus the movements produced by multiple fingers are often highly similar~\citep{Mosc08}. Thus, while multiple fingers on the same hand may increase input bandwidth on tasks that require coordinated control, they do not actually offer a large increase in the degrees of freedom available to a user~\citep{Kin09}. In addition, they may, in fact, hinder a task as they increase the chance of simultaneously selecting multiple targets~\citep{Kin09}. Finally, some research has suggested that users often do not attempt to use more than two fingers simultaneously unless prompted to~\citep{Kin09}. This too suggests that while many devices offer recognition of multiple simultaneous input points, this multiplicity is often not used or required in unimanual applications.

\subsubsection{Unimanual versus bimanual interaction}
Devices that are able to detect multiple simultaneous inputs also present the opportunity for interaction from more than just one hand. This too can increase the degrees of freedom available to a user~\citep{Kin09}.

Bimanual interaction does not suffer from the same limitations as unimanual, multifinger interaction due to the delinked nature of the input points~\citep{Mosc08}. That is, as the multiple input points come from two hands, their motion is far more seperable~\citep{Mosc08}. However, this decoupling of movement aslo means that it is more difficult to perform coordinated tasks using two hands instead of one~\citep{Mosc08}. Bimanual interactions have also been shown to have a higher miss rate than unimanual conditions~\citep{Kin09}. \citet{Mosc08} showed that bimanual interaction is preferable when performing seperable tasks but that unimanual (multifinger) interaction is prefered when attempting to perform more precise, coordinated tasks. A further constraint on bimanual manipulation is that without a clear correspondance between each hand and the control points, users often become confused~\citep{Mosc08}. This may be due to the increased cognitive load of having to control and coordinate both hands simultaneously~\citep{Kin09}. With this said, however, bimanual interaction shows a speed advantage over unimanual interaction in decoupled tasks that do not require coordinated movement~\citep{Kin09}.

The differences in the degrees of freedom, accuracy and control presented by each method suggests that these variants are not interchangeable. Thus it appears that while both unimanual and bimanual interaction have their benifits, the type of application and level of control required dictates which of the two methods is more suitable.

\subsection{Benifits}
As is apparent above, there are a number of different ways in which touch devices can be utilised. Furthermore, there does not appear to be one universally ideal technique. Instead it seems that the type of interaction style that should be used is highly application dependant. With this said, multitouch technology does offer a number of distinct benifits over traditional interactions means such as a mouse and keyboard.

One of these is that, in many cases, applications that utilise touch devices are easier for uninitiated users to learn~\citep{Mosc06}. As shown by \citet{Mosc06}, often all that is needed is the instruction to touch the device. This in turn makes applications that use touch interaction far more accessible. This may be because multi-touch interfaces have been shown to be more naturalistic, which perhaps aids learning of a novel system~\citep{Forl07}.

With regard to speed, it has been shown that the fastest multi-touch interaction (bimanual, direct touch) is roughly twice as fast as using a mouse pointer~\citep{Kin09}. Furthermore, the use of multiple fingers or hands is often able to provide task parallism and thus increase task performance~\citep{Kin09}. The increase in number of input points and degrees of freedom also means that input points can be used simultaneously to control more than just current position~\citep{Mosc06}. An example of this would be using multi-touch to combine translation and rotation of an object into one operation by utilising multiple fingers~\citep{Mosc06}.

Finally, perhaps the most important benifit of multi-touch devices, and the interaction techniques they make possible, is that they allow for a whole range of novel applications and ways in which one can interact with a computing device.

\subsection{Limitations}
As with any interaction technique, multi-touch has certain limitations when compared to other input means. These are primarily caused by the nature off touch computing, that is, users use their fingers, rather than a more precise pointer (such as a mouse cursor), to interact with objects on screen~\citep{Mosc06}.

The size of a finger when compared to that of a mouse cursor means that small items can easily become occluded when selected or interacted with~\citep{Wu03}. This has implications for the design of applications that utilise touch technology. Indirect touch methods do not suffer from this issue, however, as the finger in this case is not placed directly on the display screen~\citep{Mosc08}. While some work has been done on overcoming these issues by making use of techniques such as using a second finger to manipulate the scale or coordinate system of the display, occlusion remains a problem that must be considered within touch interface design~\citep{Mosc08}.

Another issue related to the size of a finger when compared to other pointing devices, is that of accuracy~\citep{Kin09}. Touch-based interaction has been shown to have higher miss rates when compared to mouse-based pointers~\citep{Mosc08}. In addition, the precision of movements available is greatly reduced when compared to a mouse or pen device~\citep{MoscH06}. This reduced accuracy is also not fully eliminated by the use of indirect touch methods~\citep{Mosc08}. Furthermore, due to the nature of interaction, there are limitations on the proximity and size of the display~\citep{Mosc08}. The display also fails to provide any form of haptic feedback~\citep{Wobb09}. However, this is often lacking in other interaction techniques as well~\citep{Wobb09}.

Finally, the use of more than one pointing device can also, in some cases, cause the user to become disoriented and confused due to the increased cognitive load~\citep{Mosc08}. The same has been shown to be true for multi-finger interaction when this movement is not coordinated~\citep{Kin09}.

\subsection{Conclusion}
Multi-touch technology presents the opportunity for creating applications that utilise two or more control points. This has been shown to increase task parellism, selection speed as well as change the way in which people are able manipulate and control digital objects and interfaces. However, despite these benifits, touch technology also introduces issues of occlusion and lack of accuracy. With regard to cognitive performance, while touch technology has been shown to be highly intuitive to the uninitiated, problems of increased congnitive load are introduced when using multiple fingers or hands. Therefore it appears that for multi-touch technology to be truly utilised, the application itself must suit this style of interaction. Thus touch interaction should not be viewed as a panacea. Instead one should see it as a technique that, within the right framework, can greatly improve the quality of interaction of an application.


